WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:03.600
Sales managers.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.509
Today I want to talk to you about a few things
you're doing, YOU, you are doing that's costing

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:10.750
you millions of dollars in commissions.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.779
And I'm going to speak to you from my own
personal experience and what I did in sales.

00:00:14.779 --> 00:00:17.810
I'm in Orlando right now, at, what is the
property called?

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:18.930
This is called what?

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:26.190
Caribe Royale, with top 1100 of our salespeople,
and we're sitting here, and I wrote down seven

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.900
mistakes sales managers make.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.880
And these are mistakes that I personally made
that I'm going to share with you.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.460
Let me give you the first one here.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.890
This probably is about every single one of
them that I wrote down on a sheet of paper.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:44.109
Listen, if you're in a relationship, anybody
you're with, if you're constantly afraid of

00:00:44.109 --> 00:00:49.039
losing your girl, or losing your man, you've
already lost them.

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:54.480
If you are constantly afraid of losing your
best salesperson or losing your third best

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.159
salesperson that you have in your office,
or whatever that person is, you can't lead

00:00:58.159 --> 00:00:59.579
them anymore.

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:02.789
You absolutely lost the idea of leading these
people.

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:10.000
Biggest mistake I put at number one, is when
a sales manager stops leading his people because

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.780
he became too soft of wanting to make everybody
happy.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.020
That is a very big problem.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.450
If you can't get over the fact that you're
going to lose some sales people at times,

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.710
you can't lead them better.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:21.710
Now that's number one.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:22.710
Let me give you number two.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:23.710
It kind of goes with number one.

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:26.090
There are three different types of sales managers.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.420
And I'm going to explain it to you in a way
where hopefully it will make sense to you.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:35.270
You got the communistic sales manager, you
got the socialistic sales manager, then you

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.510
have the capitalist sales manager.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:39.820
Let me tell you the energy of each one of
them.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:45.110
Because the one thing you got to judge, an
economic system on, these three economic systems

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:46.840
on is what they produce.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.439
What types of people are they producing?

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:55.759
In a communistic environment, it's about everybody
being treated equally.

00:01:55.759 --> 00:01:58.690
Which means, imagine I'm in a real estate
office and I get ten leads of somebody that

00:01:58.690 --> 00:01:59.690
wants to buy a house.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:05.490
I give one to him, one to him, one to her,
one to her, one to him, it's equally, because

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.340
everybody needs to be treated equally.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:12.900
So your worst closer who hasn't closed since
the 70s is getting the same amount of leads

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.970
as your best closer that closes 80% of the
time.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.940
You're a communistic sales leader.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:24.170
Socialistic sales leader is about being partly
fair, partly equal.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:25.170
Not fully either.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.000
They're just kind of like, I don't want to
ruffle any feathers, but I still want to make

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.540
sure my guy makes more money than the other
guy.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.820
A capitalistic sales manager does the following.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.370
Who's our biggest closer?

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:35.480
Keep feeding him.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.560
Keep feeding him.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:37.560
Keep feeding him.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.560
Keep feeding him.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:39.560
Keep feeding him.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:45.220
It's an environment where the top builders,
top salespeople, top performers are being

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:52.130
fed more than the others because this environment
produces a certain energy of competition that

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.500
the other two are not going to produce.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.960
And if you're running a sales office, if there
are no bickering, there's no fights, there's

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.900
no arguments, there's no heated, there's no
friction, there's no "I can't believe you

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:02.900
won that.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:03.900
I can't believe.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:04.900
. . how dare you!

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:05.900
I'm going to.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.510
. . " if there's none of that really going
on in a sales office where there's competition

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:11.430
going on, you have a very boring sales office.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.620
I went to a company a couple of years ago.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.940
This was a multi-billion dollar company that
invited me to speak to 64 of their executives,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:18.940
VP sales.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:24.870
I went in there and I felt like I was at one
of these, nothing wrong with Deepak Chopra,

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:28.650
I felt like I was at a Wayne Dyer or Deepak
Chopra event where everybody's holding each

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:34.130
other's hands and I love you so much, we run
a harmonious sales environment because no,

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:35.980
we don't talk like that.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.840
And they kept asking, we're trying to figure
out why are sales are down 18 months straight.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.680
I'm like, you really need another sign?

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.440
No one here is competing.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:46.900
No one is selling.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:47.900
Everyone is comfortable.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.940
You're not going to grow a business like this.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.900
What do you think it is in sports when you're
in a competitive environment?

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.040
You have to fight for that starting lineup.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.190
You have to fight to still be the point guard.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:58.820
There's a guy that could potentially take
your.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:02.900
. . how many times in football do you see
a quarterback to lose their position to somebody

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:03.900
else?

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:04.900
Is that a bad thing?

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.850
Is that not harmonious or is that threat making
the lead quarterback get better?

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:11.300
That's the whole environment of a capitalistic
environment and in sales, that works.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:16.762
If your business is flat, you're either a
communistic sales manager, or a socialistic

00:04:16.762 --> 00:04:19.880
sales manager and you need to take a personal
inventory of how you're running your business

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:20.880
there.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.040
Let's go to number three, my favorite, but
it's not number one.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.320
This is my favorite, peer pressure.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:25.660
I love peer pressure.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.010
Let me tell you.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:31.470
There's nothing more that I love in a sales
office than peer pressure.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:32.470
Nothing more.

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:38.160
There's nothing more than Larry brought six
sales in today, but Jackie brought seven.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:45.160
There's nothing more than Bobby made 193 calls
today and Larry made 217.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:49.650
There's nothing more exciting than the peer
pressure, and it's Saturday night, Bobby wants

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:53.910
to go home, it's 8:00, he wants to go and
kick it and relax and do whatever everybody

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:54.910
else is doing.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:57.400
Jackie's sitting there saying, "You go home
first!

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:58.410
You go home first!

00:04:58.410 --> 00:04:59.440
You go home first!

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:00.440
You go home first!

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:01.440
I'm staying a little longer."

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.940
He leaves at 8:15, she leaves at 8:30.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:05.760
This is exciting.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.350
This is peer pressure.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:11.470
She made him stay two more hours when he was
ready to leave at 6:00.

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:12.890
I love peer pressure.

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:18.000
A great sales office with a lot of fire in
it, there's a lot of positive peer pressure

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.000
in it.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:24.650
And peer pressure is only in a sales office
where the sales manager, the boss, the CEO,

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:29.570
whoever you call yourself, give yourself a
title, where that person allows for peer pressure

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:30.570
to be there.

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:34.340
I'm going to go to my point number four which
has to do with peer pressure.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:36.040
Listen, mistakes.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:40.491
I ran harmonious, I ran communistic/socialistic,
I'm the guy that ran.

00:05:40.491 --> 00:05:42.120
I know all this stuff because I've ran all
that stuff.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.090
It cost me a lot of money, but I adjusted.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:45.430
Right after two years, I adjusted.

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:47.440
I was 23 years old, I made the adjustment.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:49.990
Let me tell you this other part here.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.050
I love an honest office.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.570
Now what do I mean by an honest office?

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:56.639
Because you know salespeople, we, I've been
a salesperson for a long time.

00:05:56.639 --> 00:05:58.910
Oh it was like, "Oh, I made 78 calls."

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:01.870
99 calls, or it was 42 calls.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:02.870
Whatever.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:03.870
Oh, I did this much.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:04.870
I'm going to do this.

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:09.620
It's always a little bit of that lies in the
sales business that takes place.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.230
Let me tell you what I mean by honesty.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:17.080
I used to, back in the day, have every problem
come through me.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.860
So we're in the sales office, and every problem
came to me.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:21.270
I said, we're not doing this.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:22.270
I had no life.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:25.370
One day a guy comes up to me, and he said,
"Hey, Pat, this other person in the office

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:26.640
said this.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:27.650
And I think it's unfair what they did."

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:28.780
I said, "Did you tell them anything?"

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:29.780
No.

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:30.780
I said, "Why not?"

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:31.780
Well, I just didn't want to.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:32.780
. . I wanted to bring, you're the sales manager.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:36.360
I said, "No, you're unhappy with that, you
need to call them out."

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.290
You want ME to call them out?

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:39.290
"Yes.

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:40.290
Go keep them honest."

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.770
"Oh my gosh, I don't know."

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:43.050
And then I would ask, "Did you yet?"

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:45.669
"No, but he's irritating me."

00:06:45.669 --> 00:06:51.260
Either call him out, and address him and keep
him honest, or two, get over it.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.320
But I don't want to hear about it anymore.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:54.790
Because I have other things to do.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:56.220
Which one do you want to do?

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.790
Then they go address, friction, friction,
friction, boom, problem solved, come out,

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:01.540
they both gain respect for one another.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:02.870
It needs to be happening.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:04.540
Honest environment.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.430
Reports keeps everybody honest.

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:08.100
Numbers keeps everybody honest.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:13.169
Where I can see how many I did here, but I'm
doing an accountability and I know what the

00:07:13.169 --> 00:07:15.680
numbers are, those things keep everybody honest.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.419
But I want an environment where everybody
can call each other out, and everybody keeps

00:07:18.419 --> 00:07:19.419
everybody honest.

00:07:19.419 --> 00:07:20.850
The numbers keeps everybody honest.

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:26.250
A sales office that doesn't have that and
the reports aren't public, you'll generally

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:30.840
see, I'll go to real estate offices and I'll
see, that marker is a six-month marker.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:32.560
Because I know old markers.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:33.560
I know it.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:36.340
I say, that's a dying real estate office.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:43.250
A dying real estate office, a top sales office
marker, you go to it and you just smell it.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:44.290
He just wrote it today.

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:47.070
I like this office because I smell the damn
marker.

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.250
That's a sales office.

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:53.050
When I can smell the marker, because it's
being updated daily.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:57.669
It fires me up to want to compete with those
numbers if I'm a sales guy coming in.

00:07:57.669 --> 00:08:01.290
If I'm one of your sales guys, and maybe you're
watching this and your sales manager doesn't

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:02.830
do this, ask yourself, do you get fired up?

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:03.830
Do you compete?

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:05.919
When's the last time you worked a little harder
than the other guy.

00:08:05.919 --> 00:08:07.270
Because there's no competition for you.

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:08.270
You need it.

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:09.270
Honesty.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.490
Number five.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:14.860
Number five is the 90/10 rule.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:16.810
Let me tell you what the 90/10 rule is.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:20.449
Remember how initially I told you I said,
stop being afraid of losing your best salesperson

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:21.449
because you can't lead them?

00:08:21.449 --> 00:08:24.460
You officially lose the right to lead the
person, because I want to lead you.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:27.490
When I lead you, I want you to understand
I'm okay if you decide to go to a different

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:28.490
place.

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:30.830
I'm going to do my best to lead you while
you're here with me right now.

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:33.200
Sometimes coaches, oh my gosh, LeBron's on
my team.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:34.200
No!

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.779
If you're on my team, I'm leading you until
you get traded or go somewhere else.

00:08:36.779 --> 00:08:39.349
But I'm going to lead you when you're here.

00:08:39.349 --> 00:08:41.450
And if you treat players like that, they stay
forever.

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.629
Instead of saying, "Oh my gosh, what if. . " he's
leaving.

00:08:43.629 --> 00:08:45.129
He's going to another place.

00:08:45.129 --> 00:08:46.369
Here's the 90/10 rule.

00:08:46.369 --> 00:08:54.589
I would much rather take 90% of my time and
put it into one guy and teach him what I know.

00:08:54.589 --> 00:08:57.399
And then after him the next guy and teach
them what I know.

00:08:57.399 --> 00:09:01.290
And then the next guy and teach them what
I know, than take my 90% and put one here,

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:04.429
10% here, 10%, 10% here, 10% here, 10% here.

00:09:04.429 --> 00:09:06.579
. . because I'm not building a killer.

00:09:06.579 --> 00:09:08.180
Every office needs a killer.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:09.209
Every team needs a killer.

00:09:09.209 --> 00:09:10.680
Every league needs a killer.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:11.759
Every industry needs a killer.

00:09:11.759 --> 00:09:14.120
I want a killer in my office.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:17.579
If you've got a killer in your office, and
you've got 20 other agents and one's killing

00:09:17.579 --> 00:09:21.450
everybody, that killer is firing up the other
19 agents is what it's doing.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.029
You need killers in your office.

00:09:23.029 --> 00:09:28.220
the only way you produce killers is by you
putting 90% of your time on one killer.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:29.579
Then you move on to the next killer.

00:09:29.579 --> 00:09:31.439
Then you give birth to killers.

00:09:31.439 --> 00:09:36.079
Then when your office has a lot of killers,
killers only keep killers.

00:09:36.079 --> 00:09:41.660
That means that office, it's got so much peer
pressure in it that you cannot help but be

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:43.160
in there and be a killer.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:46.889
Because if you're not a killer in that office,
you're getting killed being in that office.

00:09:46.889 --> 00:09:48.470
And that's the kind of offices I like to lead.

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:51.490
That's why very few salespeople wanted to
be with me initially.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:55.860
I calmed down a little bit over the years,
but when we were hot, listen, you better come

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.459
perform when we ran offices.

00:09:58.459 --> 00:10:01.740
Now next, let's get to the next one here.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:02.740
Incentives.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:06.149
I used to do incentives, and my incentives
would be in multiple different ways.

00:10:06.149 --> 00:10:08.449
Well, you know I'm going to go out there and
I'm going to get this thing here and I'm going

00:10:08.449 --> 00:10:09.759
to give one of these things to every guy.

00:10:09.759 --> 00:10:12.959
I've given away shark teeth, I've given away
the most random things.

00:10:12.959 --> 00:10:16.920
One time I went to Canada and I bought a dinosaur
tooth for 50 bucks.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:18.819
It was the coolest thing that I gave away.

00:10:18.819 --> 00:10:19.860
It was a dinosaur tooth.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.459
Who wants a dinosaur tooth?

00:10:21.459 --> 00:10:24.069
But, you know, I gave that away.

00:10:24.069 --> 00:10:25.579
I've done a lot of different things.

00:10:25.579 --> 00:10:28.029
But eventually, eventually, this is what I
realized.

00:10:28.029 --> 00:10:33.220
If I'm spending $1,000 on incentives, instead
of doing $100, $100, $100, $100, it goes back

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:40.410
to the 90/10 rule, but this is incentives,
do 50% of your budget on one person.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:44.019
Then go, if you're doing three, 50, 30, 20.

00:10:44.019 --> 00:10:49.009
But put some of it, if you're doing $1,000,
do 25% to the number one person.

00:10:49.009 --> 00:10:51.959
Then take the 750 and put in everybody else.

00:10:51.959 --> 00:10:58.259
Make the number one guy get a gift that's
above everybody else, what it is.

00:10:58.259 --> 00:11:00.529
Make that a sexy gift.

00:11:00.529 --> 00:11:01.550
Make that a cool gift.

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:03.619
We ran a Dubai contest one time.

00:11:03.619 --> 00:11:05.819
The number one guy, I put him at Burj Al Arab.

00:11:05.819 --> 00:11:08.309
Burj Al Arab was 3500 bucks a night.

00:11:08.309 --> 00:11:09.309
Everybody else stayed at Atlantis.

00:11:09.309 --> 00:11:13.839
Atlantis was still 600 bucks a night, but
it ain't 3500 bucks a night, seven-star hotel.

00:11:13.839 --> 00:11:16.279
I remember that contest, right?

00:11:16.279 --> 00:11:20.670
If I'm running a contest and I'm giving up
a Harley or I'm giving up a Rolex, the incentives

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:24.459
at number one for my killers, have to be above
everybody else.

00:11:24.459 --> 00:11:28.970
The way you incentivize your salespeople will
determine how many killers you give birth

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:29.970
to.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:30.970
